Difference Between Delete, Truncate and Drop 

DELETE:
1. Data Manipulation Language Command (DML)
2. Delete one or more tuples of a table. 
3. Either delete all the rows in one go or can delete rows one by one.
4. It is comparatively slower than the TRUNCATE command.
5. It does not reset the high water mark (HWM) back to zero
6. Consume more resources.
7. Explicit commit is required. 

TRUNCATE: 
1. Data Definition Language Command (DDL).
2. Delete all the rows of a table in one go.
3. We can’t delete the single row as here WHERE clause is not used.
4. It is comparatively faster than the delete command.
5. Does not remove the structure of the table.
6. It resets the high water mark (HWM) back to zero.
7. Consume fewer resources.
8. Auto commit 

DROP:
1. Data Definition Language Command (DDL).
2. It is used to drop the whole table. 
3. We can drop (delete) the whole structure in one go.
4. Existence of the whole table is finished or say lost. 